Match the root nub in place, and say where the configuration thread stops

Two things go in one patch, because one round of the loop is expensive and
both of them answer questions that are open right now.

1. The root nub is matched in place.

   Run 459 settled where the chain stops.  The panel showed

       nio: CFG enq I22AP out=4 wt=3 ct=3
       nio: CFG mk ok t=0xfffffffff2c7728000

   so the configuration thread was created: kernel_thread_start returned
   success and the handle is a real thread.  And "nio: CFG alive" never
   appeared, which is the first statement in _IOConfigThread::main, before
   thread_policy_set and before the wait on the semaphore.  The thread
   exists and its body never ran.  The counters say the bookkeeping is
   right: wt is 3 and ct is 3, so the create condition was honoured and the
   four outstanding jobs are four threads' worth of work nobody is doing.

   The root nub is matched on the asynchronous path, which is exactly the
   path that needs that thread:

       } else if (!sync || (kIOServiceAsynchronous & options)) {
           _IOServiceJob::startJob( this, kMatchNubJob, options );
       } else {
           do { ... doServiceMatch( options ); ... } while (waitAgain);
       }

   The synchronous branch calls doServiceMatch() on the calling thread.
   That is the whole of the matching work and it needs no thread of its
   own.  The root nub is the one service that cannot wait -- everything
   else hangs off it: the platform expert, the storage driver, the root
   volume -- and it is the only one matched while the kernel is still
   effectively single threaded, so matching it in place costs nothing.
   doServiceMatch() sets kIOServiceConfigRunning and clears
   kIOServiceNeedConfigState, so the guard at the top of startMatching()
   keeps a second round from happening even if the configuration thread
   ever starts working.

   This is the change that moves the port forward.  Everything after it --
   the personality for the platform expert, the storage driver, the root
   volume -- is behind the root nub's match, and the root nub's match has
   been waiting on a thread that does not run.

2. The configuration thread says where it stops.

   The marks go out twice, each in its own way, because the console cannot
   be trusted to carry them.  printf goes through console_write(), which
   waits for room in the console ring when the ring stops draining, so a
   printf can block for ever and say nothing at all -- and both 456 and 459
   stopped printing right after their last line, which is what that looks
   like.  nio_u_mark() goes through cons_ops[1].putc, which the console
   blob replaced with a direct framebuffer writer: no lock, no ring, so it
   still writes when the console path is what is stuck.  The matching
   watchdog already writes that way from its own thread.

       MKOK    the thread was created            (creating side)
       ALIVE   the body was entered              (body side)
       WAIT    the body got to the semaphore     (body side)
       PICK    the body took a job off the queue (body side)

   MKOK with no ALIVE means the thread was made and never ran.  ALIVE with
   no WAIT means the body was entered and stopped before the wait.  WAIT
   with no PICK means it is blocked on the semaphore.

Nothing here changes what the kernel does beyond where the root nub's
match runs.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index 59b01d2..8f58cf9 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -1232,7 +1232,23 @@ IOService::startMatching( IOOptionBits options )
